{"name":"Viola","hex":"#966ebd","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Viola","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A taxonomic genus within the family Violaceae – violets.","A taxonomic genus within the family Hesperiidae – certain of the butterflies called skippers."]},{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A female given name from Latin.","A comune in Cuneo province, Piedmont, Italy."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Viola"}],"entries":[{"title":"Viola","blocks":[{"text":"The viola ( vee-OH-lə, Italian: [ˈvjɔːla, viˈɔːla]) is a string instrument of the violin family, and is usually bowed when played. The viola is slightly larger than the violin and has a lower sound. Since the 18th century, it has been the middle or alto voice of the violin family, between the violin (which is tuned a perfect fifth higher) and the cello (which is tuned an octave lower). The strings from low to high are typically tuned to C3, G3, D4, and A4."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cd/Bratsche.jpg/330px-Bratsche.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Viola","wikidata":"Q80284"},{"title":"Viola (plant)","blocks":[{"text":"Viola, commonly known as the violets, is a genus of flowering plants in the family Violaceae. It is the largest genus in the family, containing over 680 species. Most species are found in the temperate Northern Hemisphere; however, some are also found in widely divergent areas such as Hawaii, Australasia, and the Andes."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c8/Viola_reichenbachiana_LC0128.jpg/330px-Viola_reichenbachiana_LC0128.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Viola_(plant)","wikidata":"Q146095"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f1/Range_viola.png/500px-Range_viola.png?utm_sourc